// scripts/battle-maps.js - written by scripts/tools/make-battle-maps.js. Do not edit. Read by generator.html: the battle
// profiles' "How it was fought" (profile name: [slug, version of data/battles/<slug>.json, [[phase title, text]]]).
var BATTLE_MAPS = {
  "Battle of Adwa": ["battle-of-adwa","e6550576ce",[["The Italian columns march out in parallel","Baratieri's columns advanced towards the Ethiopian positions to reach the crests of three mountains, Albertone on the left, Arimondi in the centre and Dabormida on the right, with Ellena's reserve following behind Arimondi."],["Albertone loses his line and is charged","Albertone's column went astray and he found himself cut off from the rest of the army. His vanguard was then ferociously charged by Ethiopian units coming down from the mountains."],["Dabormida is cut off in the valley","Dabormida pushed his brigade into the valley of Mariam Shewito to relieve Albertone, away from where it was meant to go. A gap of about two miles opened, and Ras Makonnen advanced into it."],["Menelik's reserve falls on Albertone, who is routed","Menelik sent his guard against Albertone's front while Taytu's troops and Ras Mikael's troops attacked the flanks. The brigade, outflanked, began a fighting retreat that turned into a rout."],["The Ethiopians split and fall on Arimondi","After the rout the Ethiopian columns came down on Arimondi's position in two parts. One struck Mount Belah on the Italian right, and the other the junction on the Italian left, held by the 3rd Native Battalion, detached from Ellena."],["The centre gives way and Baratieri orders a retreat","Pressed at the front and outflanked on both sides, Arimondi's brigade gave way. Baratieri ordered a general retreat towards Adi Ugri and Adi Keyh, and Ras Mikael's Oromo cavalry cut down the retreating Italians."],["Dabormida's brigade is encircled","Ethiopian troops that had beaten the other brigades moved into the valley. Dabormida's final attack was repulsed with heavy losses, and as the brigade tried to withdraw it found itself effectively encircled."]]],
  "Battle of Agincourt": ["battle-of-agincourt","77e5417dcf",[["The two armies are drawn up","The English stood in three groups across the defile, with archers on the flanks and men-at-arms in the centre. The French stood in a vanguard and a main battle behind it, with dismounted men-at-arms in the wings, cavalry on the flanks of the vanguard and a mounted rearguard."],["The English move up and shoot first","Henry ordered his army forward and the stakes were pulled up and set again in the new position. The English longbowmen then shot from extreme range, and the French cavalry charged only after this first volley."],["The French cavalry charge the archers","The French cavalry, disorganised and not at full numbers, charged towards the longbowmen. They could not outflank the archers because of the woodland, and could not charge through the stakes."],["The wounded horses break back through the French","The charge failed and the cavalry fell back. Wounded and panicking horses galloped through the advancing infantry, scattering and trampling it, while the vanguard walked on through the mud."],["The French men-at-arms reach the English line","The surviving French men-at-arms reached the front of the English line and pushed it back, while the longbowmen on the flanks went on shooting at close range. When their arrows ran out the archers attacked the French with hatchets, swords, spears and mallets."],["The second line joins and the fight is lost in the press","The French second line joined the attack but was swallowed up, the narrow ground hampering it. The fighting lasted about three hours, until the leaders of the second line were killed or captured, as those of the first line had been."]]],
  "Battle of Ain Jalut": ["battle-of-ain-jalut","8b552b33a3",[["The Mongols hold the spring","Kitbuqa reached Ain Jalut first and camped by the spring, with Mount Gilboa covering one flank. Baibars, leading the Mamluk vanguard, sighted the camp from a height, while Qutuz hid the bulk of his army in the highlands."],["The Mongols advance and Baibars strikes and runs","The Mongols moved first. For many hours Baibars provoked them with hit-and-run attacks and kept most of his men out of the fight."],["The feigned retreat","After another heavy Mongol assault Baibars and his men pretended to break and rode for the highlands. Kitbuqa did not suspect a trick and followed with all his troops."],["The trap closes","When the Mongols reached the highlands the hidden Mamluks came out, shooting arrows and charging with their cavalry. The Mongols found themselves surrounded."],["The Mongols break the left, and Qutuz charges","The Mongols fought hard to break out and nearly destroyed the Mamluk left wing. Qutuz threw off his helmet so that his men could see him and led his own unit towards the weakened side."],["Flight towards Baysan","The Mongols were pushed back and fled towards Baysan with Qutuz’s men behind them. They rallied and counter-attacked, but the fight turned against them and Kitbuqa was killed with most of his army."]]],
  "Battle of Badr": ["battle-of-badr","95cf3c807e",[["The armies face each other across the valley","The Quraysh were encamped in the south-eastern part of the valley near the road from Mecca. The Muslims had come from the north and held the well nearest the Meccans."],["Duels before the lines meet","Men came out from each side to fight singly. Hamza, Ubayda and Ali met three Meccans who came out of the Quraysh ranks. Ali and Hamza each killed an opponent quickly."],["The Meccans charge","A shower of arrows from both sides followed the duels. The Meccans then took the offensive and charged upon the Muslim lines, and the Muslim duellists were carried back into the lines."],["The Muslims counter-attack","Muhammad gave the order for a counter-attack and the Muslim army rushed the Quraysh lines. Both Muslim battalions moved forward against the Meccan body."],["The Meccan lines break","The Meccans broke and ran. The fighting lasted a few hours and was over by early afternoon, after the Meccan army had scattered."]]],
  "Battle of Cannae": ["battle-of-cannae","2bca6e19d5",[["The armies draw up facing each other","The Romans faced east with the Aufidus on their right and massed their infantry deep in the centre. Hannibal put the Hispanic and Gallic infantry in the middle, the African infantry at the ends of the infantry line and the cavalry on the flanks."],["The cavalry clash and the centres advance","The battle opened with a fierce cavalry fight on both flanks. In the centre Hannibal moved the Hispanic and Gallic infantry forward in a curve, keeping the Africans back."],["The Roman cavalry is driven off","The Carthaginian horse got the upper hand on the Roman right. Hasdrubal did not chase them but rode to the other side of the field, and the allied cavalry broke between him and the Numidians."],["The centre gives way step by step","The Roman heavy infantry pressed forward and Hannibal held his men together in a controlled retreat. The curve buckled inwards as the Romans advanced and reserve lines pushed into the gaps."],["The Africans turn in and the cavalry takes the rear","The Carthaginian cavalry attacked the Roman centre from behind. Hannibal then ordered the African infantry to turn inwards against the Roman flanks, which formed a pincer."],["The Romans are enclosed and killed","Enclosed in a pocket with no means of escape, the Roman infantry was cut down by the cavalry behind it, the African infantry on both sides and the centre in front."]]],
  "Battle of Gaugamela": ["battle-of-gaugamela","76f037d638",[["The two armies stand drawn up","The Persians were already on the field. Darius stood in the centre, Bessus commanded the left wing and Mazaeus the right, with chariots in front. Alexander held the right of the Macedonians and Parmenion the left, with the phalanx between them in a double line."],["The phalanx advances and Alexander rides to the right","Alexander ordered the infantry to march towards the centre of the enemy line, with the wings echeloned back. He himself rode towards the edge of the right flank with the Companions, to draw the Persian cavalry outwards."],["The Persian wings attack","The Scythian cavalry of the Persian left tried to get round Alexander's extreme right, and a long cavalry fight followed there. Darius also sent a large part of his cavalry against Parmenion on the left."],["The chariots charge","Darius launched his chariots at the troops under Alexander. Javelin-throwers stopped many, and the Macedonian ranks opened to let the rest pass harmlessly. Those that got through were then killed."],["Alexander's wedge strikes the Persian centre","Alexander drew his Companions out of the fight, formed a large wedge with other units and led it into the Persian centre, while the phalanx kept the Persian infantry engaged. By the widely held view, Darius then broke and fled."],["Parmenion is hard pressed and a gap opens","Parmenion's wing was apparently encircled and sent desperate messages, and Alexander turned back to help instead of pursuing Darius. A gap had opened in the phalanx, and Persian and Indian cavalry broke through towards the camp."],["The raiders are dispersed and the Persian right gives way","The rear reserve phalanx attacked and dispersed the raiders as they looted. Alexander fought the Persian right-wing cavalry and Mazaeus began to pull his forces back, and they fell into disorder under the Thessalian charge."]]],
  "Battle of Gettysburg": ["battle-of-gettysburg","13ecf0e98d",[["Heth's brigades meet Buford's cavalry west of town","Buford's cavalry held the ridges west of the town to delay the Confederates. Two of Heth's brigades, part of Hill's corps, came east along the Chambersburg Pike and met his dismounted troopers."],["The I Corps arrives as the cavalry falls back","The Confederates pushed the Union cavalry east to McPherson Ridge. The vanguard of the I Corps then arrived and took up the fight beside it."],["Ewell's corps and the XI Corps come up from the north","Two divisions of Ewell's corps came down on the town from the north while the XI Corps hurried up. Rodes's and Early's divisions then assaulted and outflanked the I and XI Corps north and northwest of the town."],["The Union line falls back to Cemetery Hill","Howard ordered a retreat to Cemetery Hill, and the I Corps was driven back through the Seminary grounds and the streets of Gettysburg."],["The Union line forms a fishhook","Most of the Union infantry reached the field. The XII Corps stood on Culp's Hill, the remnants of the I and XI Corps on Cemetery Hill and the II Corps on Cemetery Ridge. Longstreet's corps came up to attack the Union left."],["Sickles's corps meets Longstreet's attack","Sickles had advanced the III Corps west to the Emmitsburg Road, away from Cemetery Ridge. Longstreet's divisions came forward and found it directly in their path."],["Pickett's Charge crosses the open ground","After a long bombardment, Pickett's division and brigades from Hill's corps stepped off the ridge and advanced to Cemetery Ridge. To the northeast Stuart's cavalry met Gregg's division and Custer's brigade, who stopped it reaching the rear of the Union infantry."]]],
  "Battle of Hastings": ["battle-of-hastings","75820f93d5",[["The two armies take their ground","The English formed a shield wall at the top of the slope. William drew up archers in front, spearmen behind them in three divisions, and kept his cavalry in reserve."],["The Normans attack uphill","The Norman archers shot uphill at the shield wall with little effect. The spearmen went forward and met a barrage of spears, axes and stones, and the cavalry came up in support without making headway."],["The Normans fall back and the English pursue","A general retreat began, blamed on the Breton division on William's left, and a rumour that William had been killed added to the confusion. Some of the English left the wall to pursue the fleeing invaders."],["William counter-attacks","William rode through his forces, showing his face and shouting that he was still alive. He led a counter-attack against the pursuing English, and the men who had left the wall were overwhelmed."],["The cavalry feign flight","After a probable lull William tried a new tactic: cavalry sent against the shield wall, then drawn back to pull the English into further pursuits. It was said to have been used twice."],["The assault on a thinned shield wall","The feigned flights did not break the line but probably thinned out the housecarls, who were replaced by men of the fyrd, and the shield wall held. Archers were used again before and during an assault by cavalry and infantry led by William."],["Harold dies and the English collapse","Harold died late in the battle, though the accounts of how he died contradict each other. His death left the English leaderless and they began to collapse, and the Normans pursued the fleeing troops."]]],
  "Battle of Hattin": ["battle-of-hattin","f9826b58f2",[["The Crusaders camp on the dry plateau","The Frankish army could not reach Tiberias by nightfall and turned towards the springs of Kafr Hattin. The Muslims placed themselves between the Franks and the water, so the Franks camped on the arid plateau, closely surrounded."],["Archers harass the Franks and the line advances","Gökböri's mounted archers shot at the Franks, who were blinded by smoke. Guy's brother Amalric formed the battle lines, and the army set out for the springs of Hattin, where Saladin's army blocked the way forward and any retreat."],["Raymond charges twice towards the lake","Count Raymond charged twice to break through to the water at Lake Tiberias. The second charge took him to the lake and he made his way to Tyre."],["The infantry flee to the Horns and Guy pitches tents","After Raymond escaped, most of the Christian infantry fled onto the Horns of Hattin and took no further part. Guy tried to pitch the tents again to block the Muslim cavalry, while the knights and mounted serjeants fought on."],["Three charges and the fall of Guy's tent","The Crusaders, surrounded, made three charges on Saladin's position. The Muslims rallied each time and drove them back to the hill, and then Guy's tent fell."]]],
  "Battle of Kadesh": ["battle-of-kadesh","65ee1a0596",[["The Hittites wait behind Kadesh","Muwatalli had placed his troops behind Old Kadesh. Ramesses had been told that the Hittite army was far off, so he was with the Amun division and his bodyguard while the Re division was still coming up to the camp."],["Hittite chariots cross the river and scatter the Re division","The Hittite chariots crossed the river and charged the middle of the Re division as it marched towards Ramesses. Caught in the open, the division scattered in all directions."],["The chariots attack the camp","The Hittite chariotry rounded to the north and attacked the Egyptian camp through the Amun shield wall, and panic spread among the Amun division. Some of the Re division fled north to the camp, pursued by Hittite chariots."],["Ramesses charges into the Hittite ranks","The momentum of the Hittite attack waned as chariots were impeded by obstacles in the camp. Ramesses defeated the first attackers and personally led several charges with his guard, some Amun chariots and survivors of the Re division."],["The Egyptian counter-attack drives them to the river","The Hittites, believing their enemies routed, stopped to plunder the camp. An Egyptian counter-attack then drove them back towards the Orontes and away from the camp, and in the pursuit lighter Egyptian chariots overtook Hittite chariots."],["A second Hittite counter-attack meets new arrivals","As the retreat reached the river, Muwatalli ordered another thousand chariots to counter-attack towards the camp. The Ne'arin from Amurru arrived and surprised them, and the Ptah division arrived from the south and threatened the Hittite rear."],["The Hittites are pinned against the Orontes","After six unsuccessful charges the Hittite forces were almost surrounded and the survivors were pinned against the Orontes. They abandoned their chariots and tried to swim the river, and many drowned. The Hittites held on to Kadesh."]]],
  "Battle of Lepanto": ["battle-of-lepanto","0440607e2f",[["The two fleets draw up in line","The Holy League fleet formed four divisions in a north-south line, its left towards the coast. The Ottoman fleet faced it with a right, a centre and a left under Uluç Ali offshore in the south."],["The galleasses open fire","The wind shifted to favour the Christians shortly before contact. Galleasses in front of their line fired at close quarters at the foremost Turkish galleys and confused their battle array."],["Barbarigo and Sirocco meet by the shore","First contact was between Barbarigo and Sirocco near the northern shore. Barbarigo tried to keep close to the coast, but Sirocco inserted galleys between his line and the shore. Barbarigo was killed, Bazán's reserve arrived and Sirocco was killed."],["The centres clash around the flagships","The centres met so hard that Ali Pasha's galley drove into the Real as far as the fourth rowing bench. Spanish Tercio infantry and Turkish janissaries fought hand to hand around the two flagships."],["The Turkish flagship is taken","Colonna came alongside the Real and mounted a counter-attack. The Turkish flagship was boarded and swept, and Ali Pasha was killed. After two hours of fighting the Turks were beaten on the left and in the centre."],["Doria sails south and Uluç Ali turns on the centre","Doria kept sailing south instead of taking his station and opened a wide gap with the centre. Uluç Ali swung round and fell on Colonna's southern flank, attacking some fifteen galleys round the flagship of the Knights of Malta."],["Bazán's reserve arrives and Uluç Ali retreats","The arrival of the reserve under Bazán kept Uluç Ali from breaking into the Christian centre. He was forced to retreat and escaped the battle with the captured flag of the Knights of Malta."]]],
  "Battle of Marathon": ["battle-of-marathon","eb54fe68af",[["The two armies form their lines","The two tribes in the Greek centre were drawn up four ranks deep and the tribes on their flanks eight deep. One explanation is that this made the Athenian line as long as the Persian line."],["The Greeks advance and the lines meet","On a signal from Miltiades the Athenians advanced and probably broke into a run at the limit of the archers' effectiveness. They went through the Persian arrows, protected for the most part by their armour, and met the enemy line."],["The Greek centre is pushed back","The fighting lasted a long time. In the centre the Persians and Sacae prevailed and broke through in pursuit inland, while on each wing the Athenians and Plataeans prevailed."],["The Persian wings collapse and the Greek wings turn in","The Athenian wings quickly routed the Persian levies on the flanks. They then turned inwards to surround the Persian centre, which had been more successful against the thin Greek centre."],["The Persian centre breaks and flees to the ships","The Persian centre broke in panic towards its ships, pursued by the Greeks. Some, unaware of the local terrain, ran towards the swamps, where unknown numbers drowned."]]],
  "Battle of Plassey": ["battle-of-plassey","814f4bdae2",[["The two armies take their ground","The Nawab's army formed an arc with the French guns at the larger tank in front. Clive lined up the Europeans in the centre with a sepoy division on each side, and posted four guns at the kilns to answer the French."],["The guns open and Clive withdraws to the grove","The French guns fired the first shot and the rest of the Nawab's artillery followed. The British lost 10 Europeans and 20 sepoys by 8:30, and Clive left the advanced guns at the kilns and drew the army back into the grove."],["Rain, and Mir Madan Khan's charge is driven back","A rainstorm drenched the Nawab's gunpowder, while the British covered theirs. Mir Madan Khan led his cavalry in a charge, but British grapeshot mortally wounded him and drove his men back."],["The Nawab's army turns back to its entrenchments","The Nawab's army stopped its cannonade and began turning back north to its entrenchments. This left St. Frais and his artillery without support."],["Kilpatrick advances and the British take the larger tank","Major Kilpatrick advanced on his own with two companies and two field pieces towards the French position. Clive brought up the rest of the army behind him, and the French artillery retreated to the redoubt."],["Mir Jafar's division turns towards the grove and is halted","The Nawab's left arm, which had lingered behind the rest, turned and marched towards the grove. Clive took it for a threat to his baggage and sent three platoons and a field piece, whose fire halted it."],["The British take the hill and the redoubt","Clive ordered a three-pronged attack on the redoubt and the hill east of it. Major Coote's grenadiers took the hill at 16:30, the redoubt fell after St. Frais retreated, and by 17:00 the British held the entrenchment and the camp."]]],
  "Battle of Stalingrad": ["battle-of-stalingrad","dab4173275",[["The 6th Army reaches Stalingrad","The 6th Army reached the outskirts of Stalingrad in pursuit of the Soviet 62nd and 64th Armies, which fell back into the city. The 4th Panzer Army had turned northwards to help take the city from the south. Romanian armies guarded the German flanks."],["The Germans push the defenders to the river","The Germans advanced into the city at great cost and pushed the Soviet defenders back into narrow zones along the west bank of the Volga."],["Operation Uranus overruns the Romanian 3rd Army","Three Soviet armies struck the northern flank of the 6th Army. The Romanian 3rd Army, with thin lines and outnumbered and poorly equipped, was overrun."],["A second attack comes from the south","A second Soviet offensive of two armies was launched south of Stalingrad against the Romanian 4th Army Corps. The Romanian infantry were overrun by large numbers of tanks."],["The ring closes at Kalach","The two Soviet forces raced west and met at Kalach, sealing the ring around Stalingrad. The 6th Army and the 4th Panzer Army were left inside it."],["The relief force advances from Kotelnikovo","Operation Winter Storm was left with the LVII Panzer Corps around Kotelnikovo, supported by the rest of the 4th Panzer Army. By 18 December it had pushed to within 48 km of the 6th Army. The trapped army made no attempt to break out or link up."],["The ring tightens and the 6th Army capitulates","Operation Koltso opened on 10 January 1943 with a very large bombardment, and the Germans retreated from the suburbs to the city. On 2 February the 6th Army capitulated, having exhausted its ammunition and food."]]],
  "Battle of Waterloo": ["battle-of-waterloo","efc17fa428",[["The armies face each other across the Brussels road","Wellington drew up his infantry just behind the crest of the ridge, with Hougoumont held ahead of his right and his heavy cavalry out of sight behind the ridge. The French formed on the slopes of another ridge to the south, symmetrically about the road."],["Hougoumont is attacked and I Corps advances","The grande batterie bombarded the centre and Reille's corps attacked Hougoumont. D'Erlon then advanced his divisions against Wellington's left wing."],["The British heavy cavalry charge","Uxbridge's two heavy brigades crossed the crest and charged downhill. I Corps was driven back across the valley in rout, and some of the cavalry went on to attack the guns of the grande batterie."],["The French cavalry attack the centre","Ney tried to break Wellington's centre with cavalry alone, sending in his reserve cavalry between Hougoumont and La Haye Sainte. Wellington's infantry formed squares against them."],["The Prussians arrive and Lobau is sent to stop them","Bülow's IV Corps came up on the French right with Plancenoit as its objective. Napoleon sent Lobau's corps to stop it."],["The Imperial Guard attacks and I Corps breaks","Napoleon committed the Imperial Guard against Wellington's centre, led by Ney. As it went forward Zieten's Prussian I Corps attacked the French right, and d'Erlon's corps broke and fled."],["The Guard retreats and Wellington advances","The Guard was repulsed and the last of it retreated. Wellington signalled a general advance and his army went forward against the retreating French."]]],
  "Fall of Constantinople": ["fall-of-constantinople","dc73de39ff",[["The armies take their positions","The Ottoman army camped outside the land walls. The European regulars lay along the walls, the troops from Anatolia south of the Lycus, and the guns and Janissaries near the Mesoteichion. Constantine held the Mesoteichion and Giustiniani the Charisian Gate to the north."],["The guns open fire and the chain holds the fleet out","Mehmed's cannons fired on the walls for weeks, but the Byzantines repaired most of the damage after each shot. The Ottoman fleet made probing attacks but could not enter the Golden Horn because of the chain."],["Ships are hauled over the hill into the Horn","Mehmed had a road of greased logs laid across Galata and dragged his ships over the hill into the Golden Horn, bypassing the chain. This threatened the supply ships from Pera and demoralised the defenders."],["The fire-ship attack on the hauled ships fails","The defenders tried to destroy the Ottoman ships in the Golden Horn with fire ships. The Ottomans forced the Christians to retreat with many casualties."],["The final assault begins at the Blachernae walls","The Christian troops of the Ottoman Empire attacked first, followed by irregular azaps and Anatolian Turkmen, who focused on a damaged section of the Blachernae walls. The Turkmen breached it and entered the city but were quickly pushed back."],["The Janissaries attack and the Genoese give way","The last wave, the elite Janissaries, attacked the walls. Giustiniani was grievously wounded and his evacuation caused panic among the defenders. His Genoese retreated into the city and towards the harbour, while Constantine's men held their ground."],["The Ottomans enter the city","The defenders were overwhelmed at several points along the wall, and the Janissaries pressed forward. The Venetians retreated to their ships, which left the Horn, and the Ottoman navy held the Golden Horn by midday."]]],
  "Third Battle of Panipat": ["third-battle-of-panipat","6c2349a698",[["The two lines face each other","The Maratha lines stood a little north of Kala Amb with Bhau and the royal guard in the centre, the Gardis on the left and Holkar and Sindhia on the right. The Afghan line stood to the south with Najib on its left and the Rohillas on its right centre."],["The Gardis advance and the Rohillas fall back","The Maratha left, under Ibrahim Khan, advanced its infantry against the Rohillas. Point-blank salvos sent the Rohillas reeling back to their lines, and the Gardi musketeers held the field for three hours."],["Bhau charges the Afghan left centre","Bhau led a charge against the forces under Shah Wali. The attack nearly broke the Afghan lines, but Shuja-ud-Daulah did not move from his position and the Maratha mounts were exhausted."],["Scindia attacks Najib and the Afghan reserve is called up","The Marathas under Scindia attacked Najib, who fought a defensive action and kept them at bay. Meanwhile Ahmad Shah called up his 15,000 reserve troops from his camp."],["The Afghan reserves charge","Fresh reserves went to support Shah Wali against Bhau in the centre. Some 7,000 Maratha cavalry and infantry were killed before the hand-to-hand fighting began."],["The Gardis are encircled","The Maratha cavalry guarding the Gardis broke its position and went out against the Rohillas. The Rohilla riflemen fired on it, then encircled the Gardis and outflanked the Maratha centre while Shah Wali pressed the front."],["Holkar and Scindia leave the field","Realising the battle was lost, Holkar's and Scindia's contingents merged, and one broke from the Maratha right and escaped south through an opening in the Durrani lines. Bhau and the royal forces fought on."]]]
};
